include "gt.pil";
include "constants_gen.pil";
include "memory.pil";
include "precomputed.pil";
include "sha256.pil";

/**
 * SHA-256 Memory Subtrace
 *
 * Virtual sub-trace to sha256.pil
 *
 * This handles the memory accesses for the SHA256Compression opcode and is virtual to the sha256_compression subtrace.
 * The sha256 compression is computed across 65 rows (64 rounds + 1 final output row). Each row represents
 * a round of sha256 compression, where the input[i] is used for the i-th round. The 1st row contains the previous hash state
 * that is read from memory. The 65th row contains the output hash state that will be written to memory.
 *
 * USAGE - Permutation
 * sel_exec_dispatch_sha256_compression { clk, space_id, output_addr, state_addr, input_addr, err }
 * is
 * sha256.start { sha256.execution_clk, sha256.space_id, sha256.output_addr, sha256.state_addr, sha256.input_addr, sha256.err };
 *
 * PRECONDITIONS:
 * - output_addr, state_addr, input_addr are resolved addresses in the valid range of the AVM memory.
 *
 * This trace reads and writes the following:
 * (1) Read State: From { state_addr, state_addr + 1, ..., state_addr + 7 }
 * (2) Read Input: From { input_addr, input_addr + 1, ..., input_addr + 15 }
 * (3) Write Output: From { output_addr, output_addr + 1, ..., output_addr + 7 }
 * All values operated on in this subtrace are validated to be U32.
 * This subtrace has a mix of a vertical and horizontal memory accesses.
 * The State and Outputs are written horizontally (i.e. there are 8 columns), this means that
 * they are loaded in "parallel" and therefore they form their own temporality groups (for the purpose of any errors)
 * The Input is "mixed" into the hash at each round (from round 1 to 16), this means the inputs are loaded in a single column and the i-th input
 * is loaded in the i-th row of computation. Therefore, a single load is a temporality group (for the purpose of errors).
 *
 * Opcode operands (relevant in EXECUTION when interacting with this gadget):
 * - rop[0]: output_addr
 * - rop[1]: state_addr
 * - rop[2]: input_addr
 *
 * Memory I/O, all operations are slice reads or writes:
 * - M[rop[0] + i]: for 0 <= i < 8, M[output_addr + i] aka output[i] (the i-th value of the hash output array)
 *     - guaranteed by this gadget to be U32.
 * - M[rop[1] + i]: for 0 <= i < 8, M[state_addr + i] aka state[i] (the i-th value of the hash state array)
 *     - guaranteed by this gadget to be U32.
 * - M[rop[2] + i]: for 0 <= i < 16, M[input_addr + i] aka input[i] (the i-th value of the hash input array)
 *     - guaranteed by this gadget to be U32.
 *
 * INTERACTIONS (as source):
 * - gt.sel_others: address range checks for state/input/output address bounds.
 *   Tuple: { max_addr, max_mem_addr, out_of_range_result }.
 * - memory.sel_sha256_op[0..7]: 8 parallel memory permutations for state reads and output writes.
 *   Tuple: { clk, space_id, address, value, tag, rw }.
 * - memory.sel_sha256_read: sequential memory reads for the 16 input values.
 *   Tuple: { clk, space_id, address, value, tag, rw=0 }.
 *
 * ERROR HANDLING:
 * Three errors need to be handled as part of this trace:
 * (1) OUTPUT_OUT_OF_BOUNDS_ACCESS: If the reads or writes would access a memory address outside
 *     of the max AVM memory address (AVM_HIGHEST_MEM_ADDRESS). This utilises the gt gadget
 *     to compare the max value of each read/write slice against AVM_HIGHEST_MEM_ADDRESS.
 * (2) INVALID_STATE_READ_TAG: If any of the state values retrieved from memory are NOT U32.
 *     This utilises the batched zero check method
 *     (see https://github.com/AztecProtocol/aztec-packages/blob/next/barretenberg/cpp/pil/vm2/docs/recipes.md#batching-comparison-of-n-bit-numbers)
 * (3) INVALID_INPUT_READ_TAG: If any of the input values retrieved from memory are NOT U32.
 *     See table below for how this is propagated
 *
 * These form the error temporality groups for Sha256 Compression. We emphasize that group (3) is not an
 * atomic temporality group but applies a per-input granularity, i.e., each input load is effectively a
 * temporality group. As soon as an invalid input tag is detected, the computation stops and the error is
 * propagated to the start row.
 *
 * TRACE SHAPE:
 * The number of rows per event depends on whether an error occurs:
 * - Normal execution: 65 rows (64 compression rounds + 1 final output row).
 * - Address out-of-range error: 1 row (end immediately on the start row).
 * - Invalid state tag error: 1 row (end on the start row after state is loaded).
 * - Invalid input tag error: 1..16 rows (one row per input loaded, end on the row
 *   with the first invalid tag).
 *
 * EXAMPLE TRACE OVERVIEW
 * Notes:
 * (1) clk, space id and output addr are propagated down all rows (so they can be accessed when writing)
 * (2) The values of mem_addr, mem_tag and registers depends on if we are at the start or end of the computation
 * (3) Input addr is incremented for the first 16 rows
 * +-------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------+
 * |       Operands and helper columns               | State Memory Columns : Reads inital state from state_addr, and writes output state to output_addr  | Input Loader: Loads 16 elements vertically | SHA256 COMPRESSION COMPUTATION |
 * +-------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------+
 * | row | clk | space id | state addr | output addr | mem addr[0] | ... | mem_addr[7]    | mem tags[8] | register[0]         | ... |     register[7]     |    input addr   | input tag | input value  |                                |
 * +-----+-----+----------+------------+-------------+-------------+-----+----------------+-------------+---------------------+-----+---------------------+-----------------+-----------+--------------+--------------------------------+
 * |  1  |  2  |     1    | 0xdeadbeef |  0xc0ffee   | 0xdeadbeef  | ... | 0xdeadbeef + 7 |    U32      | sha256_init_a[0]    | ... | sha256_init_h[7]    | 0xdecafbad      |   U32     |     456      |                                |
 * |  2  |  2  |     1    |     0      |  0xc0ffee   |     0       | ... |     0          |     0       |         0           | ... |         0           | 0xdecafbad + 1  |   U32     |     789      |                                |
 * | ... | ... |    ...   |     0      |    ...      |    ...      | ... |    ...         |    ...      |        ...          | ... |        ...          |      ...        |   ...     |     ...      |       See sha256.pil           |
 * | 16  |  2  |     1    |     0      |  0xc0ffee   |     0       | ... |    ...         |    ...      |        ...          | ... |        ...          | 0xdecafbad + 15 |   U32     |     123      |                                |
 * | ... | ... |    ...   |     0      |    ...      |    ...      | ... |    ...         |    ...      |        ...          | ... |        ...          |       0         |   ...     |     ...      |                                |
 * | 65  |  2  |     1    |     0      |  0xc0ffee   |  0xc0ffee   | ... |  0xc0ffee + 7  |    U32      | sha256_output_a[0]  | ... | sha256_output_h[7]  |       0         |    0      |      0       |                                |
 * +-----+-----+----------+------------+-------------+-------------+-----+----------------+-------------+---------------------+-----+--------------------++-----------------+-----------+--------------+--------------------------------+
 *
 * EXAMPLE INVALID TAG ERROR PROPAGATION
 * Shows how the input tag error is propagated back to the starting row from the row with the invalid tag input
 * Notes:
 * (1) err flag has to be toggled on for all rows to unconstrain the actual SHA256 Compression computation
 * +-----+--------------+---------------+-------------------+-----------------+-------+------------------------+-------------------------+-------------------------+--------+
 * | row | state addr   |  output addr  |   input addr      |  input mem tag  |  err  |  Mem Out of Range Err  |  Invalid Input Tag Err  |  Invalid Input Tag Row  |  end   |
 * +-----+--------------+---------------+-------------------+-----------------+-------+------------------------+-------------------------+-------------------------+--------+
 * | 1   |  0xdeadbeef  |  0xc0ffee     |   0xdecafbad      |     U32         |    1  |             0          |                1        |             0           |    0   | <-- Tag Error Propagated to top
 * | 2   |  0xdeadbeef  |  0xc0ffee     |   0xdecafbad + 1  |     U32         |    1  |             0          |                1        |             0           |    0   |
 * | 3   |  0xdeadbeef  |  0xc0ffee     |   0xdecafbad + 2  |     U32         |    1  |             0          |                1        |             0           |    0   |
 * | 4   |  0xdeadbeef  |  0xc0ffee     |   0xdecafbad + 3  |     U8          |    1  |             0          |                1        |             1           |    1   | <-- end only on row error
 * +-----+--------------+---------------+-------------------+-----------------+-------+------------------------+-------------------------+-------------------------+--------+
 *
 */

namespace sha256;

    pol commit sel; // @boolean
    sel * (1 - sel) = 0;

    #[skippable_if]
    sel = 0;

    // Inputs
    pol commit execution_clk;
    pol commit space_id;
    pol commit output_addr;
    pol commit state_addr;
    pol commit input_addr;

    ////////////////////////////////////////////////
    // Control flow
    ////////////////////////////////////////////////
    // Multi-row computation selectors.
    // See recipe: https://github.com/AztecProtocol/aztec-packages/blob/next/barretenberg/cpp/pil/vm2/docs/recipes.md#contiguous-multi-rows-computation-trace

    pol commit start; // @boolean
    start * (1 - start) = 0;
    pol commit end; // @boolean
    end * (1 - end) = 0;

    pol LATCH_CONDITION = end + precomputed.first_row;

    // If start or end is active, sel must be active.
    #[SEL_ON_START_OR_END]
    (start + end) * (1 - sel) = 0;

    // sel is continuous within a computation block: it cannot change except at a latch.
    #[TRACE_CONTINUITY]
    (1 - LATCH_CONDITION) * (sel - sel') = 0;

    // A new computation block must start immediately after a latch.
    #[START_AFTER_LATCH]
    sel' * (start' - LATCH_CONDITION) = 0;

    // `sel * (1 - end) = sel - end * sel` and since `end == 1 ==> sel == 1` we have `end * sel = end`.
    // Note that this is equivalent to sel * (1 - LATCH_CONDITION) because sel == 0 on the first row.
    pol NOT_END = sel - end; // @boolean

    #[CONTINUITY_EXEC_CLK] // Propagate exec clk
    (1 - LATCH_CONDITION) * (execution_clk' - execution_clk) = 0;
    #[CONTINUITY_SPACE_ID] // Propagate space id down
    (1 - LATCH_CONDITION) * (space_id' - space_id) = 0;
    #[CONTINUITY_OUTPUT_ADDR] // Propagate output addr down
    (1 - LATCH_CONDITION) * (output_addr' - output_addr) = 0;

    // Enforce input_addr increments by 1 during input rounds (first 16 rows)
    // and stays constant otherwise. This prevents malicious provers from
    // setting arbitrary memory addresses during SHA256 input loading.
    #[CONTINUITY_INPUT_ADDR]
    (1 - LATCH_CONDITION) * (input_addr' - (input_addr + sel_is_input_round)) = 0;

    ////////////////////////////////////////////////
    // Error Handling - Out of Range Memory Access
    ////////////////////////////////////////////////
    pol commit sel_state_out_of_range_err; // @boolean when start == 1 (constrained by gt lookup on start rows)
    pol commit sel_input_out_of_range_err; // @boolean when start == 1 (constrained by gt lookup on start rows)
    pol commit sel_output_out_of_range_err; // @boolean when start == 1 (constrained by gt lookup on start rows)

    // Use the comparison gadget to check that the max addresses are within range.
    // The comparison gadget provides the ability to test GreaterThan so we check
    // (1) state_addr + 7 > max_mem_addr
    // (2) input_addr + 15 > max_mem_addr
    // (3) output_addr + 7 > max_mem_addr
    pol commit max_mem_addr; // Lookup constant support: Can be removed when we support constants in lookups.
    start * (max_mem_addr - constants.AVM_HIGHEST_MEM_ADDRESS) = 0;

    // Preconditions to `gt` gadget require both inputs to be bounded by 2^128.
    // `max_state_addr`, `max_input_addr`, `max_output_addr` are sums of an address (< 2^32 by
    // addressing resolution) and a small constant, so each is < 2^33.
    // `max_mem_addr` = AVM_HIGHEST_MEM_ADDRESS which is < 2^32.
    pol commit max_state_addr; // Lookup alias support: No commit required with lookup argument aliasing.
    max_state_addr = start * (state_addr + 7); // We read 8 elements for the state
    #[CHECK_STATE_ADDR_IN_RANGE]
    start { max_state_addr, max_mem_addr, sel_state_out_of_range_err }
    in
    gt.sel_others { gt.input_a, gt.input_b, gt.res };

    pol commit max_input_addr; // Lookup alias support: No commit required with lookup argument aliasing.
    max_input_addr = start * (input_addr + 15); // We read 16 elements for inputs
    #[CHECK_INPUT_ADDR_IN_RANGE]
    start { max_input_addr, max_mem_addr, sel_input_out_of_range_err }
    in
    gt.sel_others { gt.input_a, gt.input_b, gt.res };

    pol commit max_output_addr;// Lookup alias support: No commit required with lookup argument aliasing.
    max_output_addr = start * (output_addr + 7); // We write 8 elements for the output
    #[CHECK_OUTPUT_ADDR_IN_RANGE]
    start { max_output_addr, max_mem_addr, sel_output_out_of_range_err }
    in
    gt.sel_others { gt.input_a, gt.input_b, gt.res };

    // If any of the out of range errors are set, the memory out of range error is set.
    // We gate by `start` to ensure it can only be set on this row. Any of these out of range
    // errors are not constrained on non-start rows.
    pol commit mem_out_of_range_err; // @boolean (by definition)
    mem_out_of_range_err = start * (1 - (1 - sel_state_out_of_range_err) * (1 - sel_input_out_of_range_err) * (1 - sel_output_out_of_range_err));
    // sel_state_out_of_range_err, sel_input_out_of_range_err, sel_output_out_of_range_err are boolean on start rows due
    // to the respective gt lookups and since `start` is boolean, `mem_out_of_range_err` is unconditionally boolean.

    ////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
    // Memory Operations: State and Output
    ////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
    // Since reading the hash state and writing the hash outputs have similar "shapes" (i.e. 8 columns)
    // and occur at different "times" (the start and end respectively), we define a set of
    // shared columns that we can re-use between the two. This saves us ~24 columns and 8 permutations.

    pol commit memory_address[8]; // Addresses to read from or write to
    pol commit memory_register[8]; // These will be used by state and output values
    pol commit memory_tag[8]; // Memory Tag loaded from memory
    pol commit u32_tag; // Lookup constant support: Can be removed when we support constants in lookups.
    u32_tag = sel * constants.MEM_TAG_U32;

    // When this selector is on, the memory columns are active
    pol commit sel_mem_state_or_output; // @boolean (by definition: sum of two mutually exclusive booleans)
    // Activate the memory columns if
    // (1) STATE_READ_CONDITION: start && (1 - mem_out_of_range_err)
    // (2) OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION: end && (1 - err), this uses err as it needs to capture later errors as well
    //                             (i.e. mem tags or loading inputs)

    // Simplification: start * (1 - mem_out_of_range_err) = start - start * mem_out_of_range_err.
    // Since mem_out_of_range_err can only be non-zero when start == 1 (it is gated by start in its
    // definition), we have start * mem_out_of_range_err = mem_out_of_range_err.
    pol STATE_READ_CONDITION = start - mem_out_of_range_err;

    // We use an alternative definition of OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION that involves lower degree relations.
    // Instead of defining OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION = end * (1 - err) we add the this term in the committed
    // selector sel_mem_state_or_output and define OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION = sel_mem_state_or_output - STATE_READ_CONDITION.

    // We activate these memory columns if the start or end conditions are met. Note that the two
    // conditions are mutually exclusive due to the presence of (1 - err) in the second term since
    // start && end = 1 only during an error (since errors imply end).
    #[START_OR_END_MEM]
    sel_mem_state_or_output = STATE_READ_CONDITION + end * (1 - err);

    // Effectively the same as OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION = end * (1 - err);
    pol OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION = sel_mem_state_or_output - STATE_READ_CONDITION;

    // Mem address depends on whether we are reading or writing
    memory_address[0] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * (state_addr + 0) + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * (output_addr + 0);
    memory_address[1] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * (state_addr + 1) + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * (output_addr + 1);
    memory_address[2] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * (state_addr + 2) + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * (output_addr + 2);
    memory_address[3] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * (state_addr + 3) + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * (output_addr + 3);
    memory_address[4] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * (state_addr + 4) + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * (output_addr + 4);
    memory_address[5] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * (state_addr + 5) + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * (output_addr + 5);
    memory_address[6] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * (state_addr + 6) + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * (output_addr + 6);
    memory_address[7] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * (state_addr + 7) + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * (output_addr + 7);

    // If we are at the start, the init_x values of the sha256 compression trace is populated by these registers
    // If we are at the end, the output_x_rhs values of the sha256 compression trace populate these registers
    memory_register[0] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * init_a + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * output_a_rhs;
    memory_register[1] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * init_b + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * output_b_rhs;
    memory_register[2] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * init_c + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * output_c_rhs;
    memory_register[3] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * init_d + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * output_d_rhs;
    memory_register[4] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * init_e + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * output_e_rhs;
    memory_register[5] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * init_f + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * output_f_rhs;
    memory_register[6] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * init_g + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * output_g_rhs;
    memory_register[7] = STATE_READ_CONDITION * init_h + OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * output_h_rhs;

    // Cannot just rely on end. If we error after reading the state, end will be 1 and start will be 1.
    // This will cause the reads to be interpreted as writes in the memory permutations below. We define a rw
    // flag that accounts for this.
    pol commit rw; // @boolean (by definition: equals OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION which is boolean)
    rw = OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION;
    #[MEM_OP_0]
    sel_mem_state_or_output {
        execution_clk,       space_id,
        memory_address[0],   memory_register[0],
        memory_tag[0],       /*rw=*/ rw
    } is
    memory.sel_sha256_op[0] {
        memory.clk,      memory.space_id,
        memory.address,  memory.value,
        memory.tag,      memory.rw
    };

    #[MEM_OP_1]
    sel_mem_state_or_output {
        execution_clk,       space_id,
        memory_address[1],   memory_register[1],
        memory_tag[1],       /*rw=*/ rw
    } is
    memory.sel_sha256_op[1] {
        memory.clk,      memory.space_id,
        memory.address,  memory.value,
        memory.tag,      memory.rw
    };

    #[MEM_OP_2]
    sel_mem_state_or_output {
        execution_clk,       space_id,
        memory_address[2],   memory_register[2],
        memory_tag[2],       /*rw=*/ rw
    } is
    memory.sel_sha256_op[2] {
        memory.clk,      memory.space_id,
        memory.address,  memory.value,
        memory.tag,      memory.rw
    };

    #[MEM_OP_3]
    sel_mem_state_or_output {
        execution_clk,       space_id,
        memory_address[3],   memory_register[3],
        memory_tag[3],       /*rw=*/ rw
    } is
    memory.sel_sha256_op[3] {
        memory.clk,      memory.space_id,
        memory.address,  memory.value,
        memory.tag,      memory.rw
    };

    #[MEM_OP_4]
    sel_mem_state_or_output {
        execution_clk,       space_id,
        memory_address[4],   memory_register[4],
        memory_tag[4],       /*rw=*/ rw
    } is
    memory.sel_sha256_op[4] {
        memory.clk,      memory.space_id,
        memory.address,  memory.value,
        memory.tag,      memory.rw
    };

    #[MEM_OP_5]
    sel_mem_state_or_output {
        execution_clk,       space_id,
        memory_address[5],   memory_register[5],
        memory_tag[5],       /*rw=*/ rw
    } is
    memory.sel_sha256_op[5] {
        memory.clk,      memory.space_id,
        memory.address,  memory.value,
        memory.tag,      memory.rw
    };

    #[MEM_OP_6]
    sel_mem_state_or_output {
        execution_clk,       space_id,
        memory_address[6],   memory_register[6],
        memory_tag[6],       /*rw=*/ rw
    } is
    memory.sel_sha256_op[6] {
        memory.clk,      memory.space_id,
        memory.address,  memory.value,
        memory.tag,      memory.rw
    };

    #[MEM_OP_7]
    sel_mem_state_or_output {
        execution_clk,       space_id,
        memory_address[7],   memory_register[7],
        memory_tag[7],       /*rw=*/ rw
    } is
    memory.sel_sha256_op[7] {
        memory.clk,      memory.space_id,
        memory.address,  memory.value,
        memory.tag,      memory.rw
    };

    ////////////////////////////////////////////////
    // Error Handling - Invalid State Tag (not U32)
    ////////////////////////////////////////////////
    pol commit sel_invalid_state_tag_err; // @boolean
    sel_invalid_state_tag_err * (1 - sel_invalid_state_tag_err) = 0;

    pol STATE_TAG_DIFF_0 = memory_tag[0] - constants.MEM_TAG_U32;
    pol STATE_TAG_DIFF_1 = memory_tag[1] - constants.MEM_TAG_U32;
    pol STATE_TAG_DIFF_2 = memory_tag[2] - constants.MEM_TAG_U32;
    pol STATE_TAG_DIFF_3 = memory_tag[3] - constants.MEM_TAG_U32;
    pol STATE_TAG_DIFF_4 = memory_tag[4] - constants.MEM_TAG_U32;
    pol STATE_TAG_DIFF_5 = memory_tag[5] - constants.MEM_TAG_U32;
    pol STATE_TAG_DIFF_6 = memory_tag[6] - constants.MEM_TAG_U32;
    pol STATE_TAG_DIFF_7 = memory_tag[7] - constants.MEM_TAG_U32;

    // See recipe: https://github.com/AztecProtocol/aztec-packages/blob/next/barretenberg/cpp/pil/vm2/docs/recipes.md#batching-comparison-of-n-bit-numbers

    pol BATCHED_TAG_CHECK = 2**0 * STATE_TAG_DIFF_0 + 2**3 * STATE_TAG_DIFF_1
                          + 2**6 * STATE_TAG_DIFF_2 + 2**9 * STATE_TAG_DIFF_3
                          + 2**12 * STATE_TAG_DIFF_4 + 2**15 * STATE_TAG_DIFF_5
                          + 2**18 * STATE_TAG_DIFF_6 + 2**21 * STATE_TAG_DIFF_7;

    pol commit batch_tag_inv; // @zero-check
    // See recipe: https://github.com/AztecProtocol/aztec-packages/blob/next/barretenberg/cpp/pil/vm2/docs/recipes.md#with-error-support.
    #[BATCH_ZERO_CHECK_READ]
    (STATE_READ_CONDITION * BATCHED_TAG_CHECK) * ((1 - sel_invalid_state_tag_err) * (1 - batch_tag_inv) + batch_tag_inv) - sel_invalid_state_tag_err = 0;
    // Note that STATE_READ_CONDITION == 0 ==> sel_invalid_state_tag_err == 0.

    // When writing, we need to force that the BATCHED_TAG_CHECK is zero, i.e. it is unsatisfiable for any of the tags to be != U32
    #[BATCH_ENFORCE_ZERO_WRITE]
    OUTPUT_WRITE_CONDITION * BATCHED_TAG_CHECK = 0;

    ////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
    // Memory Operation: Loading (16) Round Inputs
    ////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
    // The 16 hash inputs are mixed into the first 16 rounds of the sha256 compression.
    //
    // input_rounds_rem counts down from 16 to 0 during input rounds, then stays at 0.
    // sel_is_input_round is derived from input_rounds_rem via an unconditional zero-check:
    //   sel_is_input_round == 1 iff input_rounds_rem != 0.
    //
    // Why this works:
    // - On start: input_rounds_rem == 16, so the zero-check forces sel_is_input_round == 1.
    // - Once input_rounds_rem reaches 0: sel_is_input_round == 0, and the counter stays at 0
    //   (via #[INPUT_ROUND_CTR_DECR_COND]), so sel_is_input_round remains 0 on all subsequent rows.
    // - On inactive rows: all columns are 0, so the zero-check trivially holds with our tracegen.

    // Input Round Remaining, starting value is 16
    pol commit input_rounds_rem;
    #[INPUT_ROUND_CTR_START_COND]
    start * (input_rounds_rem - 16) = 0;
    // Decrement the counter if we are in an input round row
    #[INPUT_ROUND_CTR_DECR_COND]
    NOT_END * (input_rounds_rem' - (input_rounds_rem - sel_is_input_round)) = 0;

    pol commit sel_is_input_round; // @boolean: 1 iff input_rounds_rem != 0
    sel_is_input_round * (1 - sel_is_input_round) = 0;
    pol commit input_rounds_rem_inv; // @zero-check
    // Zero-check: sel_is_input_round == 1 iff input_rounds_rem != 0.
    // See https://github.com/AztecProtocol/aztec-packages/blob/next/barretenberg/cpp/pil/vm2/docs/recipes.md#with-error-support.
    #[SEL_IS_INPUT_ROUND_ZERO_CHECK]
    input_rounds_rem * ((1 - sel_is_input_round) * (1 - input_rounds_rem_inv) + input_rounds_rem_inv) - sel_is_input_round = 0;

    // Read input if we have not had an error from the previous temporality groups and this is an input round
    // The errors are mutually exclusive (see explanations at END_ON_ERROR below).
    pol commit sel_read_input_from_memory; // @boolean (by definition: product of booleans)
    sel_read_input_from_memory = sel * (1 - mem_out_of_range_err - sel_invalid_state_tag_err) * sel_is_input_round;

    // Put the result of the input loads in to the corresponding w value
    // The w values contain the message schedule of the sha256 compression function
    // The first 16 values correspond to the 16 input values loaded here.
    pol commit input;
    sel_read_input_from_memory * (input - w) = 0;
    pol commit input_tag;

    #[MEM_INPUT_READ]
    sel_read_input_from_memory {
        execution_clk,       space_id,
        input_addr,          input,
        input_tag,           /*rw=*/ precomputed.zero
    } is
    memory.sel_sha256_read {
        memory.clk,      memory.space_id,
        memory.address,  memory.value,
        memory.tag,      memory.rw
    };

    ////////////////////////////////////////////////
    // Error Handling - Invalid Input Tag (not U32)
    ////////////////////////////////////////////////
    // This is 1 if any of the inputs loads fail, this is propagated from the row that errored back to the start row
    pol commit sel_invalid_input_tag_err; // @boolean
    sel_invalid_input_tag_err * (1 - sel_invalid_input_tag_err) = 0;

    // This is 1 on the specific input load that produced an error
    pol commit sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err; // @boolean
    sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err * (1 - sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err) = 0;

    pol INPUT_TAG_DIFF = sel_read_input_from_memory * (input_tag - constants.MEM_TAG_U32);
    pol commit input_tag_diff_inv; // @zero-check
    // sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err == 1 iff INPUT_TAG_DIFF != 0 (zero-check with error support).
    // See https://github.com/AztecProtocol/aztec-packages/blob/next/barretenberg/cpp/pil/vm2/docs/recipes.md#with-error-support.
    #[INPUT_TAG_DIFF_CHECK]
    INPUT_TAG_DIFF * ((1 - sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err) * (1 - input_tag_diff_inv) + input_tag_diff_inv) - sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err = 0;

    // We end on rows that have a memory error, state tag error or the row that has an invalid input tag error.
    // We cannot simply end on error because we do not want to end on the propagated sel_invalid_input_tag_err.
    //
    // The three error selectors are mutually exclusive by the constraint chain:
    // - `mem_out_of_range_err == 1 ==> STATE_READ_CONDITION == 0`, which forces `sel_invalid_state_tag_err == 0`
    //   via #[BATCH_ZERO_CHECK_READ], and `sel_read_input_from_memory == 0` which forces
    //   `sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err == 0` via #[INPUT_TAG_DIFF_CHECK].
    // - `sel_invalid_state_tag_err == 1 ==> sel_read_input_from_memory == 0` (gated by (1 - sel_invalid_state_tag_err)),
    //   which forces `sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err == 0`.
    // Therefore END_ON_ERROR is boolean.
    pol END_ON_ERROR = mem_out_of_range_err + sel_invalid_state_tag_err + sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err;
    END_ON_ERROR * (end - 1) = 0; // No need to gate by `sel`, our tracegen never sets an error on an inactive row.

    // Based on the above, if sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err = 1, LATCH_CONDITION = 1.
    // If we latched BECAUSE sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err = 1, we set the propagated sel_invalid_input_tag_err = 1
    #[TAG_ERROR_INIT]
    LATCH_CONDITION * (sel_invalid_input_tag_err - sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err) = 0;

    // Propagate the value of sel_invalid_input_tag_err "up". When combined with #[TAG_ERROR_INIT], this effectively "lifts"
    // the row tag error to the starting row so we can return the error flag
    #[TAG_ERROR_PROPAGATION]
    (1 - LATCH_CONDITION) * (sel_invalid_input_tag_err - sel_invalid_input_tag_err') = 0;

    // The consolidated error flag is returned to the execution trace.
    // It encompasses mem_out_of_range_err, sel_invalid_state_tag_err and sel_invalid_input_tag_err.
    // (sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err is excluded as it implies the propagated sel_invalid_input_tag_err.)
    //
    // These three selectors are mutually exclusive.
    // Proof:
    // - mem_out_of_range_err and sel_invalid_state_tag_err are mutually exclusive with
    //   sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err (see proof at END_ON_ERROR above).
    // - When mem_out_of_range_err == 1 or sel_invalid_state_tag_err == 1: end == 1 (via END_ON_ERROR),
    //   so LATCH_CONDITION = 1, and #[TAG_ERROR_INIT] forces
    //   sel_invalid_input_tag_err == sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err == 0.
    // - When sel_invalid_input_tag_err == 1: by #[TAG_ERROR_PROPAGATION] and #[TAG_ERROR_INIT],
    //   it originates from sel_invalid_input_row_tag_err = 1 on some row, which requires
    //   sel_read_input_from_memory != 0, which requires mem_out_of_range_err = 0 and
    //   sel_invalid_state_tag_err = 0.
    pol commit err; // @boolean
    err = mem_out_of_range_err + sel_invalid_state_tag_err + sel_invalid_input_tag_err;

